            {
              "id": "SNYK-JAVA-COMFASTERXMLJACKSONCORE-19496766",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Medium severity - Deserialization of Untrusted Data vulnerability in com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "(CVE-2026-83557) com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind@2.15.4"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Overview\n[com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind](https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind) is a library which contains the general-purpose data-binding functionality and tree-model for Jackson Data Processor.\n\nAffected versions of this package are vulnerable to Deserialization of Untrusted Data in the `isSafeSubType()` method of `DefaultBaseTypeLimitingValidator`, whose denylist of unsafe base types omits `java.lang.Comparable`, so the method returns true for any subtype resolved under a `Comparable` base. An attacker can trigger instantiation of arbitrary classes that implement `Comparable`, such as `java.io.File` to reach path-traversal primitives, by sending JSON with attacker-controlled type identifiers. This requires the application to use a bare `@JsonTypeInfo` with the default validator and no explicit `PolymorphicTypeValidator`, and a property or class whose declared base type is `Comparable`.\r\n\r\n## Workaround\r\n\r\nThis vulnerability can be avoided by configuring an explicit restrictive `PolymorphicTypeValidator`, such as a `BasicPolymorphicTypeValidator` allowlist passed to `activateDefaultTyping()`, instead of relying on the default validator.\r\n\r\n**Note:** The discoverer of this vulnerability reports that \"I have not identified a class implementing Comparable that yields code execution purely through deserialization\".\n\n## Details\n\nSerialization is a process of converting an object into a sequence of bytes which can be persisted to a disk or database or can be sent through streams. The reverse process of creating object from sequence of bytes is called deserialization. Serialization is commonly used for communication (sharing objects between multiple hosts) and persistence (store the object state in a file or a database). It is an integral part of popular protocols like _Remote Method Invocation (RMI)_, _Java Management Extension (JMX)_, _Java Messaging System (JMS)_, _Action Message Format (AMF)_, _Java Server Faces (JSF) ViewState_, etc.\n\n_Deserialization of untrusted data_ ([CWE-502](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/502.html)) is when the application deserializes untrusted data without sufficiently verifying that the resulting data will be valid, thus allowing the attacker to control the state or the flow of the execution.\n  \n## Remediation\nUpgrade `com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind` to version 2.18.10, 2.21.6, 2.22.2 or higher.\n## References\n- [GitHub Advisory](https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind/security/advisories/GHSA-gx83-3vf8-gh7j)\n- [GitHub Commit](https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind/commit/eb3b7fc0f9c0d27f471550ac3316b17d1987388f)\n- [GitHub Issue](https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind/issues/6156)\n- [GitHub PR](https://github.com/FasterXML/jackson-databind/pull/6155)\n"
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-502",
                  "maven"
                ],
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 6.3,
                "security-severity": "6.3"
              }
